-- title:
--   The step index takes values in the scaled lattice
-- statement:
--   For a real number $h$, let
--
--   $$h\mathbb{Z}^d \;=\; \{\, x \in \mathbb{R}^d : \text{for every } i \text{ there is } n \in \mathbb{Z}
--   \text{ with } x_i = n h \,\} \qquad (\texttt{scaledLattice d h})$$
--
--   be the scaled lattice inside $\mathbb{R}^d$, and let
--
--   $$x_h(s) \;=\; \big(\operatorname{round}(s_i/h)\cdot h\big)_i \qquad (\texttt{stepIndex d h s})$$
--
--   be the step index at mesh $h$: coordinatewise, $s_i/h$ rounded to the nearest integer and rescaled
--   by $h$, which for $h > 0$ is the lattice point attached to $s$ by the tiling of $\mathbb{R}^d$ into
--   half-closed cubes of side $h$. Then for every point $s \in \mathbb{R}^d$,
--
--   $$x_h(s) \;\in\; h\mathbb{Z}^d .$$
--
--   The step index is the map used throughout the discretisation to replace a point by the lattice site
--   of its tile; this lemma is the well-definedness statement that lets discrete quantities indexed by
--   lattice points be evaluated at $x_h(s)$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypothesis at all is placed on $h$: the statement is asserted for every
--   real mesh, including $h \le 0$, where the tiling degenerates and both sides are junk values (for
--   $h = 0$ every coordinate of $x_h(s)$ is $0$, and $0 \cdot \mathbb{Z}^d = \{0\}$, so the membership
--   still holds). This makes the lemma usable without carrying a positivity side condition.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/LebesgueDiff.lean#L206-L208

